Fix the )LOAD timestamp printing single-digit date fields backwards.

In apl-2.0, )LOAD reports e.g. "SAVED 2026-70-30" for the 30th of July: the
month is padded on the wrong side. The workspace file itself is correct — it
stores month="7" — so only the report is wrong, and only for fields that need
padding: any single-digit month, day, hour, minute or second. It is therefore
wrong on the 3rd of a month, or before 10am, not only in July.

This is a regression introduced in 2.0, and it is still present in trunk.
Established by comparing the tags in the GNU APL svn repository:

  Archive.cc, the timestamp itself, is unchanged
      tags/apl-1.9/src/Archive.cc:1636   setfill('0') << year << "-" << setw(2) ...
      tags/apl-2.0/src/Archive.cc:1664   identical
      trunk/src/Archive.cc:1617          identical

  Command.cc gained two sticky left-justifications, new in 2.0
      tags/apl-1.9/src/Command.cc        neither line exists
      tags/apl-2.0/src/Command.cc:241    Command::do_APL_command()
                                           out << left << dec << nouppercase ...
      tags/apl-2.0/src/Command.cc:379    Command::do_APL_expression()
                                           COUT << left << dec << nouppercase ...
      trunk/src/Command.cc:273, :335     both still there

`left` is sticky, so by the time )LOAD formats its timestamp with setw(2) and
setfill('0') — which assumes the default right-justification — the fill lands
after the digit instead of before it. Archive.cc did not change; the new
left-justification in Command.cc changed the stream state it relies on.

Confirmed on a native build, so this is not specific to a WebAssembly build:
trunk compiled with clang++ on macOS prints "SAVED 2026-70-30" before this
patch and "SAVED 2026-07-30" after it, against a )SAVE that already printed
"2026-07-30" correctly. GNU APL 1.9's native binary is also correct, matching
the file comparison above.

Command.cc:1671 shows the codebase's own convention for this: it restores
`right` immediately after a left-aligned setw. )SAVE is unaffected because it
formats through Workspace::get_v_Quad_TZ().print_timestamp() (Command.cc:1069)
rather than by hand.

This patch is the minimal fix, at the point that regressed in behaviour: ask
for right-justification for the timestamp and restore left afterwards, so later
command output is unchanged. Two alternatives are arguably better upstream —
route )LOAD through print_timestamp() like )SAVE and delete the duplicated
hand-rolled formatting, or have do_APL_command()/do_APL_expression() not leave
`left` sticky, since that is a latent trap for any other setw on that stream.

--- apl-2.0/src/Archive.cc
+++ apl-2.0/src/Archive.cc
@@ -1664,14 +1664,14 @@
 const char * tz_sign = (offset < 0) ? "" : "+";
    if (!silent)   out
         << "SAVED "
-        << setfill('0') << year        << "-"
+        << setfill('0') << right       << year        << "-"
         << setw(2)      << mon         << "-"
         << setw(2)      << day         << " "
         << setw(2)      << hour        << ":"
         << setw(2)      << min         << ":"
         << setw(2)      << sec         << " (GMT"
         << tz_sign      << offset/3600 << ")"
-        << setfill(' ') <<  endl;
+        << setfill(' ') << left        <<  endl;
 
    if (!copying)
       {
